INT. FUNERAL HOME - VIEWING ROOM - AFTERNOON

Pale carpet, folding chairs in rows, a coffin at the front under a spray of white lilies. Muffled organ music from a hidden speaker. A table of finger sandwiches no one has touched.

THEODORA ACHTERBERG, 60s, black dress, stands at the casket with one hand on its edge, her face composed in the way of someone who has practiced. Her sons stand apart: the elder, GABRIEL ACHTERBERG, 40s, rigid in a dark suit, jaw clenched, by the guest book; the younger, SEBASTIAN ACHTERBERG, 30s, unshaven, tie askew, near the exit with his phone in his hand. In the second row, a stranger in a rain-soaked coat, MAEVE KIRWAN-SOTO, 30s, sits very straight with a toddler asleep against her shoulder. And at the back, leaning on a cane, the old family friend ARCHIBALD PELLEGRINI, 80s, watches them all with milky, intelligent eyes.

Sebastian glances at the stranger, and at the toddler. Gabriel follows his gaze and the muscle in his cheek jumps. Theodora does not turn her head. She smooths a ribbon on the casket with her thumb, and her other hand drifts to the edge of the lilies, crushing a petal between two fingers.

                    GABRIEL
Who invited her?

                    SEBASTIAN
Nobody.

                    GABRIEL
She can't be here.

                    SEBASTIAN
She's already here, Gabe.

Gabriel crosses the aisle in three long strides. Maeve looks up, calm. The child stirs and settles.

                    GABRIEL
I'm going to have to ask you to leave.

                    MAEVE
I came to pay respects.

                    GABRIEL
You didn't know him.

                    MAEVE
I did.

                    GABRIEL
(low)
Not like that.

                    MAEVE
Exactly like that.

Theodora crosses the room, heels silent on the carpet. She stops between them. Looks at Maeve, then at the toddler, a long and unreadable look, and then she touches the child's hair with the back of one finger.

                    THEODORA
What is her name?

                    MAEVE
Dorothea.

Theodora closes her eyes. When she opens them, they are wet and furious and kind all at once.

                    THEODORA
After his mother.

                    GABRIEL
^
Mother, don't.

                    SEBASTIAN
^
Mom, let it go.

                    THEODORA
(to the room)
Sit down. All of you.

They sit, one by one. Archibald, by the door, lowers himself slowly into a chair and sets his cane across his knees. The organ music swells and then fades. Theodora takes the empty seat beside Maeve, and puts her arm around the sleeping child's back.
